     -> It mainly inherits operations from Collection.

     -> Add Operation
          -> add(E e)
          -> addAll(Collection<? extends E> c)

     -> Remove Operation
          -> remove(Object o)
          -> removeAll(Collection<?> c)
          -> removeIf(Predicate<? super E> filter)
          -> retainAll(Collection<?> c)
          -> clear()

     -> Search / Check Operation
          -> contains(Object o)
          -> containsAll(Collection<?> c)

     -> Size Operation
          -> size()
          -> isEmpty()

     -> Iteration Operation
          -> iterator()
          -> forEach(Consumer<? super E> action)
          -> spliterator()

     -> Conversion Operation
          -> toArray()
          -> toArray(T[] a)
          -> toArray(IntFunction<T[]> generator)

     -> Stream Operation
          -> stream()
          -> parallelStream()

     -> Comparison Operation
          -> equals(Object o)
          -> hashCode()

     -> Static Factory Methods
          -> Set.of(...)
          -> Set.copyOf(Collection<? extends E> coll)

=> Set Method Concept

     -> Set extends Collection.

     -> Set mainly changes the rule of the collection:
          -> Duplicate elements are not allowed.

     -> It does not introduce index-based methods.

     -> Therefore:
          -> No get(index)
          -> No set(index, value)
          -> No indexOf()
          -> No lastIndexOf()

     -> Because Set does not represent elements using indexes.

